  {
    "episode_id": 94779,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/4/94779.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "And that was kind of my understanding of this border bill was that there was a daily trigger mark of when they would actually start to secure the border. And that was somewhere around the 7,000 mark. So once 7,000 people crossed, then they start to shut down the border. And that's more people than we saw almost the entire Trump administration crossed the border. If you do that on an annual basis and look at how many people could come across, and then how does that work? If that's in a bill that you have a trigger mark, and that becomes law, then are we permanently held to the fact that, well, we have to wait until we hit this gigantic number every day before we can actually start enforcing an actual border on our country.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "A trigger-based enforcement model implies waiting until a large threshold is met before taking action.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The border bill included a daily trigger mechanism for securing the border.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
