  {
    "episode_id": 94779,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/4/94779.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "I think that's a very profound statement. Don't compare them to the Almighty. Compare them to the alternative. And I wish we would say that more to Republicans, because I think that's a trap more for Republicans than it is for Democrats. Because, I mean, if you look at just this decision that Donald Trump made coming out on abortion and saying he's not going to sign a federal ban on abortion and i got to tell you i come from a situation where abortion crushed us in the state of michigan and i've been watching from my perspective what they're doing because i i've been you know obviously this is what crushed me i'm like okay what is their next step here and they've been for months putting articles out in the atlantic in the new york times in the and vanity fair about the woman who went to that twenty week ultrasound which is the ultrasound that insurance pays for and find something horrible and republicans would force her to have this baby and i knew the talking points were going to be more than a human being can handle and that's where i think that that discussion of he is not the almighty He cannot create morality. He cannot change culture overnight. This is not something that we should even look to politicians to do. We should be coming around the political system and saying, hey, we as Christians, we as the community, we want to talk about family. We want to talk about the importance of children and the importance of a future. But I think people have confused politicians with God and the ability to create morality. And I just think that he has taken a very tough step forward, but it's a step that takes a very, very wicked weapon away from the Democrats.",
